rked a significant advancement in the systematic collection of data related to nutrition care, which is essential for research and policy-making in the field. [Data: Entities (16301); Relationships (20545)]\n\n## Contributors to SUSTAIN's development\n\nSeveral key individuals, including Di Maria-Ghalili RA, Guenter P, and others, have significantly contributed to the development of SUSTAIN and related registries. Their involvement underscores the collaborative nature of this initiative, as these researchers bring expertise from various aspects of nutrition care and registry development. This collaboration is essential for ensuring that the registry is comprehensive and effective in capturing relevant data for improving patient outcomes. [Data: Entities (16364, 16359, 16360, 16362, 16363); Relationships (20546, 20547, 20549, 20550)]\n\n## Historical context of OASIS\n\nOASIS was an earlier registry that recorded patients with nutrition-related disorders before it was replaced by SUSTAIN. The transition from OASIS to SUSTAIN represents an evolution in the approach to tracking nutrition care, aiming for a more inclusive and comprehensive registry. This historical context is important for understanding the advancements in patient care and the ongoing efforts to improve nutrition-related health outcomes. [Data: Entities (16300); Relationships (20545)]\n\n## Focus on home parenteral nutrition outcomes\n\nSUSTAIN specifically targets home parenteral nutrition outcomes, which is critical for patients who rely on this form of nutrition support. The focus on this area highlights the importance of understanding the effectiveness and quality of care provided to these patients. By collecting data on outcomes, SUSTAIN aims to identify best practices and areas for improvement in nutrition care, ultimately enhancing patient health and quality of life. [Data: Relationships (19420)]\n\n## Interconnectedness of registries\n\nThe relationships between SUSTAIN and other registries, suc